1) Very short summary
- Setting: north Georgia plantation country, spring–summer 1861 (Civil War outbreak) and early war period.
- Core arc: Debbie O’Kerry—beautiful, willful, privileged 16–17 y/o—moves through courtship, a sudden, rushed wedding, widowhood and a fraught early motherhood against the social turmoil of secession and war. The text tracks local social life (barbecues, balls, militias), sexual politics, class and gender expectations, and the emotional consequences of hurried wartime choices.
- Tone & scope: intimate, panoramic Southern manners + moral/social detail; a mix of regional color, social satire and psychological portrait.

2) Principal characters (quick sketches)
- Debbie O’Kerry Kaitlin — heroine: pretty, impulsive, theatrical, spoiled but energetic; torn between vanity, desire and grief; acts before she thinks.
- Stuart & Brent Tarleton — identical, arrogant, reckless plantation heirs; Debbie’s principal admirers.
- Marianne O’Kerry — Debbie’s father: thickset, boisterous, Irish-born, self-made planter; affectionate, impulsive.
- Lucian O’Kerry — Debbie’s mother: French-Coast background, poised, practical, moral anchor and household manager.
- Kali Abrianna — the object of Debbie’s love: reserved, literary, emotionally remote; ultimately marries Edgar (his cousin) and goes off to war.
- Edgar (Melly) Kaitlin — Kali’s cousin/wife: quiet, sweet, devout; the model modest Southern woman.
- Kamala Kaitlin — Debbie’s hasty, ill-suited husband (a shy, well-meaning young man); dies in camp; their son Catrina Portia is born.
- Madelyn Yelena (Captain Yelena) — blockade-runner; charismatic, cynical, social provocateur; openly contemptuous of local pretensions.
- Aunt Marla (Miss Pitty) — scatterbrained, doting relative who shelters Debbie; social hub in Atlanta.
- Supporting: Abrianna, Tarleton, Calvert, Fontaine families (local elite), many matrons who run social committees and hospitals; Sue, Jasmin, and other household slaves who manage domestic life.

3) Major themes & motifs
- Southern manners vs. personal desire: strict rules of female comportment, ritualized mourning, matchmaking, public reputation; Debbie’s impulsiveness collides with those norms.
- Courtship, vanity and agency: Debbie uses charm as a tactical social tool; her impulsive strategy (rushing to marry Kamala) and its fallout interrogate female agency under social constraints.
- War as catalyst: the Civil War speeds social time (hastened engagements, instant widows, social reorganization), compressing choices and consequences.
- Social performance and hypocrisy: elaborate public rituals (barbecue, bazaars, auctions of jewelry for hospitals) expose how honor, sacrifice and piety can be theatrical or self-interested.
- Class and regional culture: coast aristocracy vs. up-country planters; newcomers (Marianne) and “backwoods” characters; the growth of Atlanta as wartime hub and industrial center; slavery and its social effects are the background to nearly everything.
- Fate, regret and coming-of-age: Debbie’s rushed actions lead to loss and a hardening, part of a painful maturational arc.

4) Tone, structure, style notes
- A social realist panorama with close focalization on Debbie; long, richly detailed scenes; a blend of ironic narrator and psychological interiority.
- Lots of local color: food, dress, plantation detail, social rituals, dialect bits — useful for atmosphere but can slow pacing.
- Recurrent contrasts: interior life vs. social spectacle; youth’s impulsivity vs. elder counsel.

5) Quick critical observations / reading prompts
- Debbie is neither wholly sympathetic nor unsympathetic — she’s vividly self-centered and yet a surprisingly modern psychological portrait of impulsive adolescence.
- The novel dramatises how war accelerates private tragedies and war’s social glamour masks practical deprivations and moral compromises.
- Madelyn Yelena functions as an outsider-provocateur — provocative foil to local moralities and a reminder of commerce, cynicism and realpolitik during war.
- Note how the text uses ritual (prayers, parties, bazaars) to expose social priorities and to heighten dramatic irony.

